WEBVTT

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.011
This first cliff, in 1956, is the introduction

00:04:32.011 --> 00:04:33.240
of the flip turn.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.269
Rather than stopping and turning around,

00:04:35.269 --> 00:04:37.113
athletes could somersault under the water

00:04:37.113 --> 00:04:39.631
and get going right away in the opposite direction.

00:04:39.631 --> 00:04:42.423
This second cliff, the introduction of gutters

00:04:42.423 --> 00:04:43.955
on the side of the pool

00:04:43.955 --> 00:04:45.512
that allows water to splash off,

00:04:45.512 --> 00:04:46.799
rather than becoming turbulence

00:04:46.799 --> 00:04:49.046
that impedes the swimmers as they race.

00:04:49.046 --> 00:04:50.685
This final cliff,

00:04:50.685 --> 00:04:51.871
the introduction of full-body

00:04:51.871 --> 00:04:53.768
and low-friction swimsuits.

00:04:53.768 --> 00:04:57.457
Throughout sports, technology has
changed the face of performance.

00:04:57.457 --> 00:05:00.233
In 1972, Eddy Merckx set the record